      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Alterations in lipids in muscle and plasma have been documented in insulin-resistant people with obesity. Whether these lipid alterations are a reflection of insulin resistance or obesity remains unclear."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Nondiabetic sedentary individuals not treated with lipid-lowering medications were studied (n\u2009=\u200951). Subjects with body mass index (BMI)\u2009>\u200925 kg/m(2) (n\u2009=\u200928) were stratified based on median glucose infusion rate during a hyperinsulinemic-euglycemic clamp into insulin-sensitive and insulin-resistant groups (above and below median, obesity/insulin-sensitive and obesity/insulin-resistant, respectively). Lean individuals (n\u2009=\u200923) served as a reference group. Lipidomics was performed in muscle and plasma by liquid chromatography electrospray ionization-tandem mass spectrometry. Pathway analysis of gene array in muscle was performed in a subset (n\u2009=\u200935)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In muscle, insulin resistance was characterized by higher levels of C18:0 sphingolipids, while in plasma, higher levels of diacylglycerol and cholesterol ester, and lower levels of lysophosphatidylcholine and lysoalkylphosphatidylcholine, indicated insulin resistance, irrespective of overweight/obesity. The sphingolipid metabolism gene pathway was upregulated in muscle in insulin resistance independent of obesity. An overweight/obesity lipidomic signature was only apparent in plasma, predominated by higher triacylglycerol and lower plasmalogen species."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Muscle C18:0 sphingolipids may play a role in insulin resistance independent of excess adiposity."
          }
        ]
      },
